<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Problem Definition</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to imprint an imperceptible holistic watermark <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> during the generation process of a latent diffusion model for an image <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and comprehensively leverage the intrinsic patterns of this watermark to achieve reliable and robust copyright protection and tampering localization within a unified framework.
Let the LDMs follow a generative process parameterized by <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. The watermark embedding task can then be formulated as:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathcal{G}_{\phi}(z,W)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathcal{G}_{\phi}(z,W),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the LDM-based image generation function, and <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is the latent input. The embedded watermark <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> remains imperceptible yet robust against various tampering manipulations <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, such as copy-paste or splicing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To detect tampering and recover the watermark, we employ a forensic network <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes the potentially manipulated image <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as input and simultaneously produces the extracted watermark <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and a tampering localization map <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{W},\hat{M}=\mathcal{F}_{\psi}(T(X))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W},\hat{M}=\mathcal{F}_{\psi}(T(X)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to be reliably recovered with its integrity preserved, while <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math> precisely identifies the manipulated regions, ensuring a robust and effective framework for both watermark extraction and tampering detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Comparison of Existing Methods</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a concise overview of the key comparison methods, summarized in Tab. <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Comparison of Existing Methods ‣ StableGuard: Towards Unified Copyright Protection and Tamper Localization in Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. As illustrated, this work focuses on two primary tasks: tampering localization and copyright protection for content generated by diffusion models. The compared copyright protection approaches encompass both traditional post-hoc image watermarking techniques and more recent diffusion-native watermarking methods that integrate directly with the generative process. Tampering detection methods are categorized into passive localization and proactive protection strategies. Our proposed approach offers a unified, diffusion-native solution for both tampering detection and copyright protection, achieving state-of-the-art performance across both tasks.</p>
</div>
